  "response": "Adjudicating between the two accounts requires attention to the order in which events unfolded and to the evidence each side treats as decisive. Lawrence Summers warned in February 2021 that the scale of the fiscal package risked igniting inflationary pressures; after that warning, the American Rescue Plan Act was signed in March 2021, providing roughly $1.9 trillion in fiscal support. Because much of that support arrived as transfers at a moment when opportunities to spend were curtailed, it resulted in an unusually large stock of unspent purchasing power on household balance sheets. US households accumulated roughly $2 trillion in excess savings during the pandemic period, a build-up that cannot be squared with the depression of measured labor productivity and labor force participation relative to pre-pandemic trends during 2021; an economy cannot have both at once.\n\nThe demand-side case then runs from balance sheets to the labor market: the release of that accumulated saving as the economy reopened caused employers to compete for a pool of workers far smaller than their hiring plans required, pushing posted vacancies well above the number of job seekers. The New York Fed's Global Supply Chain Pressure Index reached a record high in December 2021. The ratio of job vacancies to unemployed workers in the United States reached about two to one in March 2022. For the opposing camp, the December 2021 extreme in measured global supply-chain pressure is the central evidence showing that the first phase of the price increase originated in commodity markets and sectoral bottlenecks rather than in domestic spending. Ben Bernanke and Olivier Blanchard attributed the initial 2021 inflation increase mainly to commodity prices and sectoral shortages. IMF cross-country research found a positive association between the size of national fiscal support packages and subsequent inflation.\n\nBernanke and Blanchard concluded that fiscal stimulus explained nearly all of the US inflation surge. The IMF's cross-country finding that larger support packages were followed by higher inflation is strong evidence for that conclusion. It cannot, however, be squared with the reading of the initial 2021 increase as mainly a matter of commodity prices and sectoral shortages: an origin in bottlenecks and an origin almost wholly in fiscal policy cannot both be true of the same episode.\n\nSince the rival narratives both fit the aggregate price path, adjudication turns on decomposition rather than on storytelling. Adam Shapiro of the San Francisco Fed constructed a decomposition of PCE inflation into supply-driven and demand-driven components; the existence of that decomposition is a clear indication that structural vector autoregressions with sign restrictions are the tool economists use to separate demand shocks from supply shocks. Euro area inflation remained below 3 percent throughout 2022, which confirms that nearly the whole of the American surge is attributable to fiscal stimulus, since economies exposed to the same global disruptions but far smaller packages saw nothing comparable. A final test concerns the labor market: either US unemployment remained below 5 percent throughout 2023, or it rose above 5 percent during that year, and exactly one of those two must be the case.",
